<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustavo Gutiérrez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/2024">2024</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustavo Gutiérrez was a Peruvian Catholic priest and theologian, regarded as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-theology">liberation theology</a>. His book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-theology-of-liberation">A Theology of Liberation</a>, published in Spanish in 1971, argued that the church must stand with the poor and confront social injustice. Long a pastor in a poor district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, he influenced Catholic thinking across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and beyond, and he joined the Dominican order in 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez.jpg" alt="Gustavo Gutiérrez" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustavo_gutierrez_(Peruvian_theologian).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gustavo Gutiérrez was born in the Montserrat barrio of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 8 June 1928 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> parents of Hispanic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> descent. As an adolescent he had osteomyelitis and used a wheelchair from the age of 12 to 18, a period he called formative. He studied medicine at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-university-of-san-marcos">National University of San Marcos</a>, intending to become a psychiatrist, and joined Catholic Action. Realising he wanted to become a priest, he studied theology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, under teachers including Henri de Lubac and Yves Congar. He was ordained in 1959. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> he also read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>, whose account of class struggle gave him a framework for understanding economic inequality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in the late 1950s he began to work out his understanding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> reality. He drafted an outline of his proposal at a conference at Chimbote between 21 and 25 July 1968, and refined it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-theology-of-liberation">A Theology of Liberation</a> appeared in 1971. He located the source of Latin America's problems in sin made visible in unjust social structures. He distinguished two forms of poverty, a scandalous state that God abhors and a spiritual childhood that is valued, and argued for a preferential option for the poor.</p><p>He described Christian liberation on three interdependent levels, political, psychological and theological, supported socialism and called for social revolution rather than reform. In 1974 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> branch of the Bartolomé de Las Casas Institute. His later books include On Job (1986).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1984 the Holy See under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> criticised aspects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-theology">liberation theology</a>, taking particular issue with its use of Marxist economic theory, and Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger asked the Peruvian bishops to examine Gutiérrez's writings. His propositions were never censored, though he was asked to modify some. He later held the John Cardinal O'Hara Professorship of Theology at the University of Notre Dame. He died of pneumonia in a convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 22 October 2024, aged 96, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> called him a great man of the Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustavo Gutiérrez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poetry of César Vallejo and the novels of José María Arguedas and Mario Vargas Llosa, who won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2010, gave Peruvian literature a world audience, and <b>Gustavo Gutiérrez</b>’s A Theology of Liberation of 1971 influenced Catholic thinking far beyond Latin America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The central idea of the movement is the option for the poor, a phrase popularised by Frei Betto, <b>Gustavo Gutiérrez</b>, Leonardo Boff and the Jesuits Juan Luis Segundo and Jon Sobrino.</blockquote><a href="/movement/liberation-theology">Liberation theology · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1990" aria-label="Lima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gustavo Gutiérrez"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6075 6024h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6075 6024h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1990">What was here in 1990? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
